<h2>Common Rowlett claims</h2>
<h3>Tornado and wind damage</h3>
<p>Tornado claims often involve the roof, framing, windows, garage doors, siding, fences, outbuildings, and contents, plus additional living expenses when a home is unlivable. Straight-line winds can do similar damage on a smaller scale. Early, thorough documentation is essential. See <a href="/services/tornado-damage-claims/">tornado damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Hail on suburban roofs</h3>
<p>Brick homes with composition shingle roofs are common here. Hail bruises shingles and dents gutters, vents, and metal trim, and insurers sometimes approve only part of the roof. Our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page covers what to photograph.</p>
<h3>Flooding and water damage</h3>
<p>Heavy rain can flood streets near Rowlett Creek and low-lying areas. Rising surface water is flood, which standard homeowners policies generally exclude; NFIP or private flood policies cover it. Water entering through storm damage or a plumbing failure may be covered under your homeowners policy. See <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

